/*@

\chapter{Calling Convention}
\label{call}

The compiler and VM agree on a particular treatment of argument and
return values, so that callable objects can be invoked from call sites
which may be oblivious to their signatures. This is the \textit{calling
  convention}; every function and call site adheres to it.


\section{Callee}

The ordered list of a function's formal arguments, their names and their
types, along with its return type, comprises its signature
(\autoref{callable:sig}).

When called, the code of a function expects an [[argbits_t]] as a first,
hidden, argument; furthest below [[bp]] in the stack frame
(\autoref{vm:frame). Each bit in this value corresponds to a formal
argument of the callee; it's set if the caller has provided the
corresponding actual argument.

Arguments are expected in the order, and with the types, specified by
the signature's [[.args]] array. Arguments of scalar type are expected
to be unboxed into the stack frame, and may undergo arithmetic promotion
(\autoref{scalar:type}). An omitted argument will have been given its
zero value.

On return, the function's code indicates the value of the specified
[[.ret_type]] that the caller should take as its result. When this is a
scalar, it's not boxed.


\section{Caller}

The expression that calls a function can provide its actual arguments in
a number of ways. Each can be \textit{named}, \textit{omitted}, the
\textit{rest} vector ``[[...]]''; or the default, \textit{positional}.

Before the call proceeds, the actual arguments must be \textit{matched}
with the formal arguments of the callee.

\subsection{Fast Call}
\label{call:fast}

When the compiler knows the signature of the function being called, it
can use the \textit{fast call} convention (the call must also not
require allocation of a rest vector, but this is an implementation
limitation and could be relaxed without difficulty.)

Arguments are matched at compile-time, and opcodes generated to assign
their values directly to the appropriate slots in the stack frame of the
callee. Scalars passed in this manner are left unboxed. Since the return
type is known, scalar return values don't need boxing either.

\subsection{Universal Call}
\label{call:uni}

The \textit{universal call} convention is used in all other cases.

Actual arguments are supplied by sub-opcodes, with the same names and in
the same order as the call expression. Argument matching (and
rest-vector creation, when necessary) occurs at run-time. Values are
passed as reference objects; scalars must be boxed before being used in
such a call. The result of a call is also expected to be a reference
object; scalars will be boxed upon return.


\section{Argument Matching}
\label{call:match}

The argument matching process is defined in its own module, used by both
compiler and VM.

==rt/call.c*/
